## 5. `sitemap.ts` — both apps

Create the file at `app/sitemap.ts`. Next.js auto-generates the `/sitemap.xml` route from it.

### apps/web — `app/sitemap.ts` (static)

The portfolio is mostly static — sections are listed manually.

```ts
import type { MetadataRoute } from 'next';

export default function sitemap(): MetadataRoute.Sitemap {
  const siteUrl = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL!;
  return [
    {
      url: siteUrl,
      lastModified: new Date(),
      changeFrequency: 'monthly',
      priority: 1,
    },
    // Add section anchors or additional routes as they are created:
    // { url: `${siteUrl}/#projects`, changeFrequency: 'monthly', priority: 0.8 },
  ];
}
```

### apps/blog — `app/sitemap.ts` (dynamic)

Includes the blog home + all published posts.

`export const revalidate` is required so that posts published after the last build appear in the sitemap without needing a redeploy. 24 hours is a reasonable interval for a weekly-cadence blog — the sitemap is a crawl signal, not the primary discovery path for new posts (that is handled by the SSR fallback in `dynamicParams`).

When traffic or post volume grows significantly, replace the time-based `revalidate` with on-demand revalidation (`revalidatePath('/sitemap.xml')`) triggered from the admin on publish — but that is out of scope for now.

The API enforces a max limit of 30 items per page — paginate to collect all posts.

```ts
import type { MetadataRoute } from 'next';
import { fetchPosts } from '@/modules/posts/services/posts.service';

// Regenerates the sitemap every 24 hours.
// For a weekly-cadence blog this is sufficient — new posts are discoverable
// immediately via SSR fallback; the sitemap is updated for crawlers within a day.
export const revalidate = 86400;

export default async function sitemap(): Promise<MetadataRoute.Sitemap> {
  const siteUrl = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL!;

  const first = await fetchPosts({ page: 1, limit: 30 });
  const remaining = await Promise.all(
    Array.from({ length: first.meta.totalPages - 1 }, (_, i) =>
      fetchPosts({ page: i + 2, limit: 30 })
    )
  );
  const allPosts = [first, ...remaining].flatMap((r) => r.items);

  const postEntries: MetadataRoute.Sitemap = allPosts
    .filter((p) => p.published && p.publishedAt)
    .map((post) => ({
      url: `${siteUrl}/${post.slug}`,
      lastModified: new Date(post.updatedAt),
      changeFrequency: 'weekly',
      priority: 0.8,
    }));

  return [
    {
      url: siteUrl,
      lastModified: new Date(),
      changeFrequency: 'weekly',
      priority: 1,
    },
    ...postEntries,
  ];
}
```

