## 8. Markdown-rendered content (`PostDetailMarkdown`)

Post content is rendered via `react-markdown` in `packages/modules/src/posts/components/PostDetailMarkdown.tsx`. This introduces two SEO-specific concerns.

### 8.1 Images in Markdown — replace native `<img>` with `next/image`

The current `img` custom renderer uses a native `<img>` tag. Replace it with `next/image` using `fill` + a relative container so that images benefit from lazy loading, format optimization (WebP/AVIF), and CLS prevention without requiring explicit dimensions (which Markdown images never have).

```tsx
// packages/modules/src/posts/components/PostDetailMarkdown.tsx
import Image from 'next/image';

// inside the components prop:
img: ({ src, alt }) => (
  <figure className="mt-5 sm:mt-6">
    <div className="relative w-full overflow-hidden rounded-lg" style={{ aspectRatio: '16 / 9' }}>
      <Image
        src={src!}
        alt={alt ?? ''}
        fill
        sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 760px"
        className="object-contain"
        // No priority — content images are always below the fold
      />
    </div>
    {alt && (
      <figcaption className="mt-2 text-center text-xs text-neutral/60">{alt}</figcaption>
    )}
  </figure>
),
```

**`rehypeRaw` caveat:** The component uses `rehypeRaw`, which allows raw HTML in the Markdown source. A raw `<img>` tag written directly in the post content will **bypass the custom renderer** and render as native HTML. Authors must use Markdown image syntax (`![alt](url)`) — not raw `<img>` — for `next/image` to apply.

Make sure the external image domain used in post content is declared in `next.config.ts` under `remotePatterns` (same domain as `ogImageUrl` likely covers this).

### 8.2 Heading hierarchy — avoid duplicate `h1`

The `PostDetailMarkdown` component already renders a styled `h1` when the Markdown source contains `# Heading`. If the post page also renders the post title inside an `<h1>`, there will be **two `h1` elements on the same page**, which harms SEO.

**Rule:** The post page renders the title in an `h1`. Therefore, post authors must **never start their Markdown content with a `#` first-level heading**. Content should start from `##` (h2) downward.

Document this as an authoring convention in the admin — the post title field is the `h1`; the body starts at `h2`.

